Chronic Pain / Poorly Managed Pain
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
Pain duration >3 months, Sleep disturbances, Significant mood alterations, Functional/social withdrawal

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-011
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of chronic pain with severe psychological distress and insomnia.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a 72-hour period of escalating, unrelenting neuropathic pain, total sleep deprivation, and acute suicidal ideation related to pain distress. The assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period due to the imminent risk of self-harm, severe psychological decompensation, and the need for immediate, titratable pharmacological intervention to break the pain-insomnia cycle.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: The pain is tearing through my nerves, I haven't slept in three days, and I just cannot endure another minute of this.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports an 18-month history of continuous pain following a remote lumbar injury. The current exacerbation began 3 days ago following minor physical exertion (gardening), which triggered a severe flare. The patient describes profound insomnia and non-restorative sleep, obtaining zero hours of rest over the past three nights. This has led to severe generalized fatigue, significant mood alterations including acute despair, and complete functional/social withdrawal from all normal activities today.
Functional Status: Bedbound currently due to severe pain and fatigue.
Social/Safety Concerns: Acute distress and hopelessness; high risk for medication misuse or self-harm due to pain fatigue.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/92 mmHg | HR: 108 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 20 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 85.0 Kg
General: Patient appears exhausted, tearful, and physically guarded, shifting constantly in bed to find comfort.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal exam reveals diffuse tenderness across the lumbar paraspinals without focal neurological deficits. Straight leg raise is negative bilaterally. Skin is intact. Affect is profoundly depressed and anxious. Examination confirms a pain duration >3 months with an intensity disproportionate to the mild paraspinal muscle spasm currently observed.
Environmental Observations: Bedroom is heavily darkened; multiple half-empty bottles of over-the-counter analgesics on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: Regular Pregabalin and Amitriptyline doses checked; patient has escalated PRN paracetamol/ibuprofen use without relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Chronic Pain / Poorly Managed Pain (with acute pain crisis and insomnia) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Lumbar Disc Herniation - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of new focal motor weakness, normal bowel/bladder function, and negative straight leg raise.

Clinical Depression (Primary) - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary driver, because the mood alterations strictly follow and correlate with the severity of the mechanical pain flare.

Fibromyalgia - Ruled out/unlikely due to the pain remaining localized to the original lumbar injury site, lacking the widespread, symmetric tender points characteristic of fibromyalgia.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Acute psychological distress)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of a severe pain crisis, accompanied by 72 hours of total sleep deprivation and severe mood alteration, represents a decompensated chronic pain state. The objective findings of tachycardia, elevated blood pressure driven by distress, and physical exhaustion confirmed that the patient was experiencing an acute physical and psychiatric crisis. This assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period because immediate medical intervention was required to safely administer acute anxiolysis/sedation, break the severe insomnia cycle, and prevent acute self-harm or accidental analgesic overdose.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Diazepam 5mg for acute skeletal muscle relaxation and to facilitate sleep. Advised immediate cessation of all unprescribed over-the-counter NSAIDs.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you practice progressive muscle relaxation for 10 minutes before bed, you decrease your baseline central nervous system arousal; therefore, completing this routine daily lowers your overall pain threshold over time. When you walk for exactly 5 minutes each morning regardless of pain, you prevent further muscle deconditioning; rewarding yourself with a preferred activity immediately after reinforces this essential pacing habit.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a multidisciplinary chronic pain clinic.
DME/Safety (Optional): Recommended a specialized lumbar support cushion for seating.
Follow-up: Telehealth GP review tomorrow afternoon to assess sleep quality and mood stability.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department or call 000 if you develop sudden weakness in your legs, lose control of your bladder or bowels, or experience uncontrollable thoughts of ending your life.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Acute flares in chronic pain are self-limiting but highly distressing; the goal is return to baseline, not total pain eradication.
Clinical Course: The medication will force the nervous system to rest tonight. The mechanical flare will likely take 7-10 days to return to the baseline chronic pain level.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek urgent medical care if you find yourself taking more medication than prescribed or if you feel completely overwhelmed and unable to keep yourself safe.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The diazepam acts as a circuit breaker, relaxing the tight muscles and allowing your brain to finally initiate the sleep cycle. Sleep is critical, as a sleep-deprived brain amplifies pain signals.
Actionable Steps: Take the diazepam now and attempt to sleep. Implement the 5-minute morning walk. Stop taking extra over-the-counter pills.

